# Hypothesis (post-hoc, pattern + labelled interpretation; no causal claim)

Prevalence of non-native plants introduced via gardening varies strongly and non-randomly among the ten habitat
types of the Barcelona province, and the direction depends on which denominator is used, so both layers are reported
together. Considering only the 191 of 632 plots that actually hold non-native plants (conditional layer; region-wide
169/481 records = 35.1%), the gardening share of the non-native pool is highest in **Wetlands (55.8%, 29/52, Wilson
95% CI 42.3-68.4)** and **Coastal (53.5%, 23/43, CI 38.9-67.5)**, intermediate in **Riparian (36.5%, CI 27.5-46.4)**,
**Meadows (30.8%)** and **Urban (29.8%, 45/151, CI 23.1-37.5)**, and lowest in **Croplands (18.5%, 20/108, CI
12.3-26.9)**; habitat as a whole is associated with the gardening share (binomial GLM, likelihood-ratio chi2 = 50.6
on 9 df, asymptotic p = 8.2e-8, permutation p = 0.0002 from 5000 habitat-label shuffles). After Holm correction over
the 15 contrasts that met the >=10-records rule, three differences stand: Wetlands vs Croplands (OR 5.55,
p_holm < 0.0001), Coastal vs Croplands (OR 5.06, p_holm = 0.0006) and Wetlands vs Urban (OR 2.97, p_holm = 0.018);
Riparian vs Croplands is suggestive but not significant after correction (OR 2.52, p_holm = 0.054). Taking all 632
plots as the denominator (unconditional layer: share of plots in a habitat holding at least one gardening species),
the ranking changes because encounter rate differs by an order of magnitude: **Urban 47.7% (CI 36.0-59.6), Wetlands
50.0% (34.8-65.2), Coastal 44.8% (28.4-62.5), Riparian 36.1% (25.2-48.6), Croplands 20.7% (13.6-30.0)**, then
Meadows 7.7%, Coniferous forests 6.5%, Broad-leaved forests 4.4%, Scrublands 1.9% and **Rock outcrops 0% (0/57, CI
0-6.3)**; this association is even stronger (LR chi2 = 146.9, 9 df, permutation p = 0.0002; 24 of 45 pairs
Holm-significant). For Broad-leaved forests (4/4 records gardening), Coniferous forests (7/8), Scrublands (2/5) and
Rock outcrops (0/1) the habitat-level record totals are 1-8, so their apparent conditional prevalence (100%, 87.5%,
40%, 0%) is descriptive only and its direction is not interpreted: Rock outcrops does not mean "no gardening
introductions", it means one non-native record in 57 plots. Interpretation (explicitly an interpretation, not a
tested result): the pattern is consistent with gardening-derived species being the dominant component of the
non-native pool where the overall non-native load is small and propagulation comes from nearby ornamental stock
(waterways, wetlands, coasts), whereas in croplands the pool is dominated by the unintentional seed-contaminant
pathway (77% of its records); the two layers jointly imply that urban and wetland habitats are where gardening
species are most likely to be encountered at all, while cropland records are least gardening-derived. This is a
post-hoc description of the supplied data, not a confirmatory test, and no causal verb is intended: the design
contains no survey-effort variable and habitat is nearly collinear with the urbanisation gradient
(mean urban cover 1000 m buffer: Urban 0.50 vs Meadows 0.03), so habitat cannot be separated from that gradient here.
